maries (NCpedia returned 403)","source_url":"https://scencyclopedia.org/sce/?p=6725","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-02","kind":"road","name":"Forbes Road","year_open":1758,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Hulbert 1903","kind_as_r7":"military road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1758,"year_note":"Cut as a military road in 1758; settler route after 1763. Upgrade as Pennsylvania State Road 1791 rests on one source","other_names":"Pennsylvania State Road; Glade Road","endpoint_a":"Carlisle PA","endpoint_b":"Pittsburgh (Fort Pitt) PA","unverified_note":"1791 state-road date unverified (single source); Hulbert not opened in this pass (Gutenberg unavailable)","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/41118","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-03","kind":"road","name":"Braddock's Road","year_open":1755,"date_quality":"unverified","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Wikipedia n.d. (lead only); Armao n.d.","kind_as_r7":"military road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1755,"year_note":"Cut in 1755; stopped short of Fort Duquesne after Braddock's defeat","endpoint_a":"Cumberland MD (Fort Cumberland)","endpoint_b":"Monongahela River near Pittsburgh PA","native_path_basis":"Washington's 1754 road (its predecessor) followed the Nemacolin Trail (Armao)","unverified_note":"Only encyclopedic summaries seen; endpoints and later use need a primary or NPS source","source_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes_Road","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-04","kind":"road","name":"Crown Point Road","year_open":1760,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Crown Point Road Association n.d.","kind_as_r7":"military road","year_basis":"wagon","wagon_note":"widened for wagons 1760 (R7)","year_open_r7":1760,"year_note":"Cut 1759, widened for wagons 1760; described as the New England settler route into Vermont after 1763","other_names":"Crown Point Military Road","endpoint_a":"Charlestown NH (Fort at No. 4)","endpoint_b":"Crown Point NY","unverified_note":"Post-1763 settler use is a general statement only","source_url":"https://www.crownpointroad.org/home/?p=94","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-05","kind":"road","name":"Wilderness Road","year_open":1796,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Longfellow n.d.; Armao n.d.; NPS n.d.","kind_as_r7":"road","year_basis":"wagon","wagon_note":"wagon road 1796 (NPS, FHWA); 1795 per Speed via Armao","year_open_r7":1775,"year_authorized":1779,"year_note":"Blazed 1775 (Boone for Henderson's Transylvania Co.); Virginia road act 1779; wagon road 1795 (Speed, quoted by Armao) or 1796 (NPS, FHWA)","other_names":"Boone's Trace; Boone's Road","endpoint_a":"Long Island of the Holston (Kingsport TN)","endpoint_b":"Boonesborough KY (branch toward Crab Orchard)","via":"Cumberland Gap; Martin's Station; Dix River fork","native_path_basis":"\"a constellation of worn Indian footpaths and buffalo trails\" (Armao)","nations_on_route_as_named":"Cherokee (Sycamore Shoals 1775, see R1 T1775)","unverified_note":"Wagon-road year 1795 vs 1796; Long Island of the Holston start from secondary summaries only","source_url":"https://history.ky.gov/kentucky-ancestors/the-untraveled-history-of-the-wilderness-road","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-06","kind":"road","name":"Avery's Trace","year_open":1788,"date_quality":"verified","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Smith 2017","kind_as_r7":"road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1788,"year_authorized":1787,"year_note":"NC road act 1787; by 1788 still a rough trail marked by blazed trees","other_names":"Old North Carolina Road; Emery Road","endpoint_a":"South end of Clinch Mountain (East TN)","endpoint_b":"Nashville TN","via":"Clinch River near Oak Ridge; Winter's Gap; Emory River near Wartburg","native_path_basis":"Northern route followed sections of Tollunteeskee's Trail","nations_on_route_as_named":"Cherokee (demanded tolls)","source_url":"https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/entries/historic-trails/","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-07","kind":"road","name":"Walton Road","year_open":1802,"date_quality":"verified","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Smith 2017","kind_as_r7":"road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1802,"year_authorized":1795,"year_note":"1795 act for a wagon road Knoxville–Nashville; made a turnpike 1801; 'broad and commodious' by 1802","endpoint_a":"Knoxville TN","endpoint_b":"Nashville TN","source_url":"https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/entries/historic-trails/","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-08","kind":"road","name":"Philadelphia and Lancaster Turnpike","year_open":1795,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"FHWA n.d. (Rakeman 1795)","kind_as_r7":"turnpike","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1795,"year_note":"Construction start given as 1792 or 1793; opened 1795. First long-distance stone-and-gravel road built to formal plans (FHWA)","other_names":"Lancaster Pike","endpoint_a":"Philadelphia PA","endpoint_b":"Lancaster PA","unverified_note":"Construction start year disputed (1792 vs 1793)","source_url":"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/rakeman/1795.htm","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-09","kind":"road","name":"Great Genesee Road","year_open":1797,"date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Greene 1925","kind_as_r7":"road","year_basis":"r7_year_open","year_open_r7":1797,"year_authorized":1794,"year_note":"State act 22 Mar 1794 (Old Fort Schuyler to Genesee River); $2,200 for improving it 1797; Utica–Geneva stage 1797; extension to Buffalo by law 1798 (secondary)","other_names":"Genesee Road; State Road","endpoint_a":"Utica NY (Old Fort Schuyler)","endpoint_b":"Geneva NY (1797); Genesee River; Buffalo (from 1798)","via":"Cayuga Ferry; Canandaigua","native_path_basis":"Williamson called it \"little better than an Indian trail\" (secondary quote)","nations_on_route_as_named":"Oneida; Onondaga; Cayuga; Seneca (Haudenosaunee lands; see R1)","unverified_note":"Act date, stage date and 1798 extension seen only in search summaries of county histories; Greene 1925 confirms the 1797 $2,200","source_url":"https://schenectadyhistory.org/resources/mvgw/history/080.html","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-10","kind":"road","name":"Mohawk Turnpike","date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Greene 1925","kind_as_r7":"turnpike","year_authorized":1800,"year_note":"Incorporated 1800 and work began; opening year not found","endpoint_a":"Schenectady NY","endpoint_b":"Utica NY","unverified_note":"Opening year blank","source_url":"https://schenectadyhistory.org/resources/mvgw/history/080.html","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-11","kind":"road","name":"Seneca Road","date_quality":"partial","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Greene 1925","kind_as_r7":"turnpike","year_authorized":1800,"year_note":"Chartered 1800 running west from Utica; took over the Utica–Canandaigua section of the Genesee Road (secondary)","other_names":"Seneca Turnpike","endpoint_a":"Utica NY","endpoint_b":"Canandaigua NY","unverified_note":"Western endpoint and opening year from secondary sources only","source_url":"https://schenectadyhistory.org/resources/mvgw/history/080.html","todo":"hand-trace","clipped_to_extent":false},"type":null},{"properties":{"id":"R7-12","kind":"road","name":"Great Western Turnpike","date_quality":"unverified","source":"R7 (roads_R7.csv)","reference":"Greene 1925; New York State Library n.d.","kind_as_